name: awk
boss: Indexer
flavor: Zero-indexed. Off by one and you get nothing.
brief: awk splits every line into fields and runs your program on each one.
brief: $1 is the first field, $NF the last, $0 the whole line. NR is the line
brief: number. That is most of it.
brief:
brief: A program is pattern { action }. Leave off the pattern and it runs on
brief: every line; leave off the action and it prints the line. BEGIN runs
brief: before the first line, END after the last -- which is where totals go.
brief:
brief: Where cut takes columns, awk takes columns AND does arithmetic, keeps
brief: counters, and holds a map. That is why it outlives cut in your fingers.
---
q: print the third field of every line in %log%
a: awk '{print $3}' %log%
out: ^INFO$
ref: awk
teach: awk
---
q: print the last field of every line in %log%
a: awk '{print $NF}' %log%
out: .
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: NF is the number of fields, so $NF is the last one
---
q: print the first and third fields of %log%, separated by a space
a: awk '{print $1, $3}' %log%
out: 2024-03-01 INFO
ref: awk
teach: awk
---
q: print each line of %log% with its line number in front
a: awk '{print NR, $0}' %log%
out: ^1 2024
ref: awk
teach: awk
---
q: count the lines in %log% using awk
a: awk 'END {print NR}' %log%
out: ^10$
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: END runs once, after the last line, and NR is still set
---
q: print only the lines of %log% that mention %sev%
a: awk '/%sev%/' %log%
out: %sev%
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: a pattern with no action means "print the line"
---
q: print the second field of the lines of %log% mentioning %sev%
a: awk '/%sev%/ {print $2}' %log%
out: [0-9]
ref: awk
teach: awk
---
q: print the lines of %csv% using a comma as the field separator, showing field 1
a: awk -F, '{print $1}' %csv%
out: ^gateway$
ref: awk
teach: awk -F
---
q: print field 1 of %csv% skipping the header row
a: awk -F, 'NR > 1 {print $1}' %csv%
out: ^gateway$
ref: awk
teach: awk -F
hint: NR is the line number, so NR>1 means "not the first line"
---
q: add up the fourth column of %csv%, skipping the header
a: awk -F, 'NR > 1 {s += $4} END {print s}' %csv%
out: ^75$
ref: awk
teach: awk -F
---
q: print the largest value in the fourth column of %csv%
a: awk -F, 'NR > 1 && $4 > m {m = $4} END {print m}' %csv%
out: ^42$
ref: awk
teach: awk
---
q: count how many times each %sev% level appears in %log%
a: awk '{c[$3]++} END {for (k in c) print k, c[k]}' %log%
out: [0-9]
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: awk arrays are maps -- c[$3]++ counts per distinct value
---
q: print only the first occurrence of each third field in %log%
a: awk '!seen[$3]++' %log%
out: INFO
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: the counter is zero the first time, which is false, which prints
---
q: print rows of %csv% where the status field is up
a: awk -F, '$3 == "up"' %csv%
out: gateway
ref: awk
teach: awk
---
q: print rows of %csv% whose count field is above 10
a: awk -F, 'NR > 1 && $4 > 10' %csv%
out: 42
ref: awk
teach: awk
---
q: print field 1 of %csv% with a header line of your own printed first
a: awk -F, 'BEGIN {print "host"} {print $1}' %csv%
out: ^host$
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: BEGIN runs before the first line is read
---
q: pass the shell variable value %word% into awk as a variable called w and print it
a: awk -v w=%word% 'BEGIN {print w}'
out: %word%
ref: awk
teach: awk -v
---
q: print field 3 of %csv% with the output fields joined by a colon
a: awk -F, -v OFS=: '{print $1, $3}' %csv%
out: gateway:up
ref: awk
teach: awk -v
hint: OFS is the output field separator
---
q: print the number of fields on each line of %csv%
a: awk -F, '{print NF}' %csv%
out: ^4$
ref: awk
teach: awk
---
q: give awk its program with the explicit program flag rather than positionally
a: awk -e '{print $1}' %log%
out: 2024-03-01
ref: awk
teach: awk -e
